" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/f2beba76039ead47c83c93aaad5f9c4717a4abd2b803e65157f23d770f4f9181.png"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/state.json" "file_sha256" "01ae30657cc075b3bf827e699e81b7553edf9f4a4101aca1cb230f5da63669f8" "basis" 1230 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-160621" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/4a78a3e98da4c602b4e8a2ebbe8860f75e507ec2ec5c20df0e59100f96a099ac.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/state.json" "file_sha256" "01ae30657cc075b3bf827e699e81b7553edf9f4a4101aca1cb230f5da63669f8" "basis" 1230 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-160371" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(6,-1),(-4,1),(3,5).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{180}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${180}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n- $(-6,-1)$\n- $(4,-1)$\n- $(3,-5)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/state.json" "file_sha256" "0941a3fc630c49049616f7dac844f2d71597c194b13fe3e18e0fafa725d616a4"} {"question" "q-160371" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{180}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${180}^{∘}$ is given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{{180}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{{180}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 & -4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-6,1),(4,-1),(-3,-5).$ \n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/state.json" "file_sha256" "0941a3fc630c49049616f7dac844f2d71597c194b13fe3e18e0fafa725d616a4"} {"question" "q-160371" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4a94f00ef0d1589267e275ebae786a4898c09a90a8bed2c5352a2c73a403dd5c"} {"question" "q-160371" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/state.json" "file_sha256" "0941a3fc630c49049616f7dac844f2d71597c194b13fe3e18e0fafa725d616a4"} {"question" "q-160371" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/state.json" "file_sha256" "0941a3fc630c49049616f7dac844f2d71597c194b13fe3e18e0fafa725d616a4"} {"question" "q-160371" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/state.json" "file_sha256" "0941a3fc630c49049616f7dac844f2d71597c194b13fe3e18e0fafa725d616a4"} {"question" "q-81819" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(4,4),(-3,-3),(-1,7).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{180}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${180}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n- $(3,-3)$\n- $(-4,-4)$\n- $(1,-7)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/state.json" "file_sha256" "0941a3fc630c49049616f7dac844f2d71597c194b13fe3e18e0fafa725d616a4"} {"question" "q-81819" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{180}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${180}^{∘}$ is given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{{180}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{{180}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 3 & -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-4,-4),(3,3),(1,-7).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/state.json" "file_sha256" "0941a3fc630c49049616f7dac844f2d71597c194b13fe3e18e0fafa725d616a4"} {"question" "q-81819" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4a94f00ef0d1589267e275ebae786a4898c09a90a8bed2c5352a2c73a403dd5c"} {"question" "q-81819" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/state.json" "file_sha256" "0941a3fc630c49049616f7dac844f2d71597c194b13fe3e18e0fafa725d616a4"} {"question" "q-81819" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/state.json" "file_sha256" "0941a3fc630c49049616f7dac844f2d71597c194b13fe3e18e0fafa725d616a4"} {"question" "q-81819" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/state.json" "file_sha256" "0941a3fc630c49049616f7dac844f2d71597c194b13fe3e18e0fafa725d616a4"} {"question" "q-160374" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(4,-3),(2,-2),(1,3).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation ${R}_{-\\pi }$ by an angle of $\\pi $ radians about the origin?\n\n- $(-4,3)$\n- $(-2,2)$\n- $(-1,-3)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/state.json" "file_sha256" "0941a3fc630c49049616f7dac844f2d71597c194b13fe3e18e0fafa725d616a4"} {"question" "q-160374" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The clockwise rotation ${R}_{-\\pi }$ by an angle of $\\pi $ radians is the same as counterclockwise rotation by the same angle. It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{-\\pi },$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{-\\pi }$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-4,3),(-2,2),(-1,-3).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/state.json" "file_sha256" "0941a3fc630c49049616f7dac844f2d71597c194b13fe3e18e0fafa725d616a4"} {"question" "q-160374" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4a94f00ef0d1589267e275ebae786a4898c09a90a8bed2c5352a2c73a403dd5c"} {"question" "q-160374" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/state.json" "file_sha256" "0941a3fc630c49049616f7dac844f2d71597c194b13fe3e18e0fafa725d616a4"} {"question" "q-160374" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/state.json" "file_sha256" "0941a3fc630c49049616f7dac844f2d71597c194b13fe3e18e0fafa725d616a4"} {"question" "q-160374" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/state.json" "file_sha256" "0941a3fc630c49049616f7dac844f2d71597c194b13fe3e18e0fafa725d616a4"} {"question" "q-160618" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-2,-1),(2,-3),(1,5).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{180}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${180}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n- $(2,1)$\n- $(-2,3)$\n- $(-1,5)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/state.json" "file_sha256" "0941a3fc630c49049616f7dac844f2d71597c194b13fe3e18e0fafa725d616a4"} {"question" "q-160618" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{180}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${180}^{∘}$ is given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{{180}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{{180}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(2,1),(-2,3),(-1,-5).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/state.json" "file_sha256" "0941a3fc630c49049616f7dac844f2d71597c194b13fe3e18e0fafa725d616a4"} {"question" "q-160618" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4a94f00ef0d1589267e275ebae786a4898c09a90a8bed2c5352a2c73a403dd5c"} {"question" "q-160618" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/state.json" "file_sha256" "0941a3fc630c49049616f7dac844f2d71597c194b13fe3e18e0fafa725d616a4"} {"question" "q-160618" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/state.json" "file_sha256" "0941a3fc630c49049616f7dac844f2d71597c194b13fe3e18e0fafa725d616a4"} {"question" "q-160618" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/state.json" "file_sha256" "0941a3fc630c49049616f7dac844f2d71597c194b13fe3e18e0fafa725d616a4"} {"question" "q-12121" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-1,-2),(-1,2),(1,1).$  Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation ${R}_{-\\pi }$ by an angle of $\\pi $ radians about the origin?\n\n- $(1,2)$\n- $(1,-2)$\n- $(-1,-1)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/state.json" "file_sha256" "0941a3fc630c49049616f7dac844f2d71597c194b13fe3e18e0fafa725d616a4"} {"question" "q-12121" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The clockwise rotation ${R}_{-\\pi }$ by an angle of $\\pi $ radians is the same as counterclockwise rotation by the same angle. It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{-\\pi },$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{-\\pi }$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(1,2),(1,-2),(-1,-1).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I, II and III.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/state.json" "file_sha256" "0941a3fc630c49049616f7dac844f2d71597c194b13fe3e18e0fafa725d616a4"} {"question" "q-12121" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4a94f00ef0d1589267e275ebae786a4898c09a90a8bed2c5352a2c73a403dd5c"} {"question" "q-12121" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/state.json" "file_sha256" "0941a3fc630c49049616f7dac844f2d71597c194b13fe3e18e0fafa725d616a4"} {"question" "q-12121" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/state.json" "file_sha256" "0941a3fc630c49049616f7dac844f2d71597c194b13fe3e18e0fafa725d616a4"} {"question" "q-12121" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/state.json" "file_sha256" "0941a3fc630c49049616f7dac844f2d71597c194b13fe3e18e0fafa725d616a4"} {"question" "q-81820" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/assets/e6741c165a0f0f50bf932dd04c6d4c1157b4f70a8dc4c63efc5416b3883124bc.png)\n\nConsider the vector $v$ shown above. Find the vector $Av$ given that \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/state.json" "file_sha256" "0941a3fc630c49049616f7dac844f2d71597c194b13fe3e18e0fafa725d616a4"} {"question" "q-81820" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of the Euclidean plane ${R}^{2}$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of ${i,j}$ under $A$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =A(i)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-i \\\\ {j}^{′} & =A(j)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/assets/4e0e5c9cee037bded1f5739af35c3a8acfaa4d4e6f469b3e50afe69b96990e39.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of $\\pi $ radians (counterclockwise or clockwise) about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ (counterclockwise or clockwise) by an angle of $\\pi $ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/assets/45f0ff53440247c533dd2b97ae03c8cd3e2e762b67832ce770c0bd5749e74f6e.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/state.json" "file_sha256" "0941a3fc630c49049616f7dac844f2d71597c194b13fe3e18e0fafa725d616a4"} {"question" "q-81820" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-ca